Audio: stop msm_afe_hw_params writing past the end of its DMA buffer.

This is a kernel panic that any PipeWire or PulseAudio system will hit within
seconds of the sound card appearing. Android never hits it, which is why it
shipped.

THE PANIC, captured on gts7fewifi 2026-08-15 via /sys/fs/pstore
--------------------------------------------------------------

    Internal error: Oops: 96000047 [#1] PREEMPT SMP
    CPU: 5 PID: 4210 Comm: wireplumber
    pc : __memset+0x16c/0x188
    lr : msm_afe_hw_params+0xc8/0x19c [platform_dlkm]
    x0 : ffffffc02de58000    (destination)
    x1 : 0000000000000000    (fill byte)
    x2 : 00000000004fffc0    (length: 5242816 bytes)
    Call trace:
      __memset
      snd_soc_component_hw_params
      soc_pcm_hw_params
      snd_pcm_hw_params
      snd_pcm_common_ioctl
      snd_pcm_ioctl
      do_vfs_ioctl
    Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception

ESR 0x96000047 decodes as a data abort, DFSC 0x07 (translation fault, level 3)
with WnR set, so it is a WRITE to an unmapped page: the memset ran off the end
of the mapping. The mapped region was 0x100000 bytes; the memset was asked for
0x4fffc0.

WHY IT HAPPENS
--------------
techpack/audio/dsp/q6afe.c, q6afe_audio_client_buf_alloc_contiguous():

        if (ac->port[dir].buf) {
                pr_debug("%s: buffer already allocated\n", __func__);
                return 0;
        }

It returns SUCCESS without allocating when a buffer already exists for this
direction, and it does not look at the requested size while doing so.

techpack/audio/asoc/msm-pcm-afe-v2.c, msm_afe_hw_params():

        rc = q6afe_audio_client_buf_alloc_contiguous(dir, prtd->audio_client,
                (params_buffer_bytes(params) / params_periods(params)),
                params_periods(params));
        ...
        buf = prtd->audio_client->port[dir].buf;
        dma_buf->area  = buf[0].data;                    /* OLD, small buffer */
        dma_buf->bytes = params_buffer_bytes(params);    /* NEW, large size   */
        ...
        memset(dma_buf->area, 0, params_buffer_bytes(params));

So the SECOND hw_params on a substream, asking for a bigger buffer than the
first, keeps the first allocation and then zeroes the new, larger size into it.

ALSA explicitly permits repeated SNDRV_PCM_IOCTL_HW_PARAMS on the same open
substream, and that is exactly how a session manager probes a device: open,
refine, try a size, try another. WirePlumber does it to every PCM it finds, so
the card cannot survive being enumerated. Android's audio HAL sets its buffer
once and never grows it, so the bug is invisible there.

Note the constraints make a large request legal: the capture PCM advertises
buffer_bytes_max = MAX_CAPTURE_PERIOD_SIZE * MAX_CAPTURE_NUM_PERIODS =
24576 * 384 = 9437184 bytes, so a 5 MB buffer is a perfectly valid thing for
userspace to ask for.

THE FIX
-------
Refuse the request instead of corrupting memory. -ENOMEM from hw_params is a
normal, non-fatal answer: snd_pcm_hw_params() propagates it to userspace, which
retries with smaller parameters, which is what a probing session manager does
anyway.

Deliberately NOT freeing and reallocating the buffer here. The DSP already has
this memory mapped through msm_audio_ion, and tearing that down inside
hw_params, on a substream that may be mid-probe, is a much larger change than
this bug justifies.

The first hw_params is unaffected: the allocation is made with exactly the
requested size, so alloc_bytes equals params_buffer_bytes(params) and the check
passes. Only a LATER, larger request is refused.

VERIFY AFTER FLASHING
---------------------
    # with the card up, this used to panic the kernel within seconds:
    systemctl --user restart wireplumber   # or: pkill wireplumber
    dmesg | grep -i "only .* allocated"    # expect the refusal, not a panic
    speaker-test -c2 -twav -l1             # expect audio, no reset

--- a/techpack/audio/asoc/msm-pcm-afe-v2.c
+++ b/techpack/audio/asoc/msm-pcm-afe-v2.c
@@ -764,6 +764,7 @@
 	struct snd_dma_buffer *dma_buf = &substream->dma_buffer;
 	struct pcm_afe_info *prtd = runtime->private_data;
 	struct afe_audio_buffer *buf;
+	size_t alloc_bytes;
 	int dir, rc;
 
 	pr_debug("%s:\n", __func__);
@@ -796,6 +797,28 @@
 		mutex_unlock(&prtd->lock);
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * q6afe_audio_client_buf_alloc_contiguous() returns 0 WITHOUT
+	 * allocating when a buffer already exists for this direction, and it
+	 * ignores the requested size while doing so. A second hw_params asking
+	 * for a bigger buffer therefore keeps the first, smaller allocation,
+	 * and the memset below then writes past the end of it and panics the
+	 * kernel. ALSA allows repeated hw_params on an open substream and that
+	 * is exactly how a session manager probes a device, so this is
+	 * reachable by any unprivileged process that can open the PCM.
+	 *
+	 * Refuse rather than corrupt memory. -ENOMEM is a normal answer here;
+	 * userspace retries with smaller parameters.
+	 */
+	alloc_bytes = (size_t)buf[0].size *
+		      prtd->audio_client->port[dir].max_buf_cnt;
+	if (params_buffer_bytes(params) > alloc_bytes) {
+		pr_err("%s: asked for %u bytes but only %zu allocated\n",
+		       __func__, params_buffer_bytes(params), alloc_bytes);
+		mutex_unlock(&prtd->lock);
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	}
 
 	pr_debug("%s:buf = %pK\n", __func__, buf);
 	dma_buf->dev.type = SNDRV_DMA_TYPE_DEV;
